    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d663">

                <pb facs="0703" n="605" xml:id="pg605"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.5034 Business Tax/52: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China
                        (Johnson)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-09-29T16:00:00-05:00">September 29, 1932—4
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>336. Your despatch 1669, August 4, and telegram 1061, September 3, 3 p.m.
                    Department’s instruction 62 of February 20, 1931, 5 p.m.,<anchor n="57"
                        xml:id="d663-f1"/> to the effect that the business tax can not be considered
                    applicable to American firms, and Department’s instruction 85 of March 6, 1931,
                    5 p.m.,<anchor n="58" xml:id="d663-f2"/> with regard to consular officers
                    pointing out to American firms, who may apply to them for advice, practical
                    aspects of the situation as indicated in your 93, March 1, 1931, 3 p.m.,<anchor
                        n="59" xml:id="d663-f3"/> section 5, remain in force.</p>
                <p>Department notes that Hankow Consulate General will await actual attempt to
                    collect the tax from American firms before making further representations and
                    believes that you should do likewise.</p>
                <p>Department also desires that you endeavor to cooperate with your interested
                    colleagues in deciding upon a uniform course of action.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
